# lto-enabled-list for deepin/UOS.
#
# Source packages listed here are the only ones built with LTO by
# default (dpkg vendor build feature optimize/lto, supported on
# amd64 / arm64 / sw64 / loong64). Every source package absent from
# this list is built without LTO unless its own packaging opts in
# explicitly (optimize=+lto). Read by Dpkg::Vendor::Debian from this
# fixed path: /usr/share/lto-enabled-list/lto-enabled-list.
#
# format: <source> any | <arch> [<arch> ...]
#   any         build the source package with LTO on every supported
#               architecture
#   <arch> ...  build it with LTO only on the listed architectures
#   lines starting with '#' are comments
#
# Entries are added only for source packages whose LTO rebuild is
# proven, tagged with the architecture(s) where that was observed.
# The initial entries below are the update-lto-20260910 rebuild
# campaign (deepin-community project 818): the 619 source packages
# with a merged "Rebuild with LTO enabled" pull request (618
# original batch plus a 12-package supplementary batch from
# 2026-09-12), tagged any. Narrow an entry to specific
# architectures, or remove it entirely, when a rebuild on an
# architecture fails.
#
# Not listed, although their campaign pull requests are merged:
#   - boost1.83, kwin, lapack, openexr: later confirmed broken;
#   - binutils, cdrkit, gnustep-make, libnfs, x264: their
#     debian/rules hardcode CFLAGS and never consume the dpkg build
#     flags, so the injected LTO flags cannot reach the compiler.
# Source packages outside deepin-community are not listed either;
# add them once their LTO rebuild is proven and tracked.
#
# The qt6-* entries below are redundant by design: deepin builds the
# Qt6 stack with LTO by default (explicit optimize=+lto in
# deepin-community qt6-* packaging); they are listed only because
# the rebuild completed.
